Understanding Your First Response

Every Raptor response is a structured message. Here's what each field means.

Response anatomy

{
  "ok": true,
  "reply": "The deployment is verified. Binary hash matches...",
  "decision_type": "respond",
  "trust_boundary": {
    "primary": "verified",
    "sublabel": "1 segments verified",
    "actionability": { "level": "safe", "label": "All claims verified" },
    "provenance": {
      "execution_id": "abc-123",
      "input_hash": "sha256:..."
    }
  },
  "segments": [
    {
      "boundary": "confirmed",
      "text": "Binary hash matches the build attestation.",
      "basis": "attestation"
    }
  ],
  "decision_support": {
    "confidence": "CLEAR",
    "recommendation": "Deployment integrity confirmed.",
    "basis": "Ed25519 attestation verified against signer trust registry."
  },
  "audit_trail": [
    { "name": "intent_received", "time": "17:44:23 UTC", "status": "pass" },
    { "name": "governance_evaluated", "time": "17:44:23 UTC", "status": "pass" },
    { "name": "response_generated", "time": "17:44:24 UTC", "status": "pass" },
    { "name": "response_attested", "time": "17:44:24 UTC", "status": "pass" }
  ]
}

Trust boundary levels

| Level | Letter | Meaning ||-------|--------|---------|| Confirmed | C | Verified against an authoritative source || Executed | X | Action ran with recorded result || Retrieved | R | Fetched from a system of record || Likely | L | High-confidence inference, not verified || Uncertain | U | Limited evidence, treat with caution |

Segments

A single AI response often contains multiple claims. Raptor does not assign one trust level to the entire response -- it classifies each claim independently.

The segments array breaks the response into individually governed pieces:

"segments": [
  {
    "boundary": "confirmed",
    "text": "Binary hash matches the build attestation.",
    "basis": "attestation"
  },
  {
    "boundary": "likely",
    "text": "The deployment should complete within

TypeScript SDK

The @raptor/sdk package is a thin, typed fetch wrapper around the Raptor REST API. It preserves trust-boundary types end-to-end — from the API response through your application code — with zero runtime dependencies.

1. Install

npm install @raptor/sdk

Requires Node.js 18+ (uses the built-in fetch API).

2. Initialize the client

import { RaptorClient } from "@raptor/sdk";

const raptor = new RaptorClient({  baseUrl: "https://raptor.harpyits.com",  auth: { type: "access_token", token: "rpt_your_key" },});

The SDK supports multiple auth modes:

// Clerk JWT (browser or server)
auth: { type: "clerk", getToken: () => clerk.session.getToken() }

// Static Clerk tokenauth: { type: "clerk", token: "eyJhbG..." }

// API keyauth: { type: "access_token", token: "rpt_a1b2c3d4..." }

// Founder keyauth: { type: "founder_key", key: "your_founder_key" }

3. Send a governed message

const response = await raptor.chat("What are the risks of deploying on Friday?");

console.log(response.reply);console.log(response.trust_boundary.primary); // "inferred" | "confirmed" | ...console.log(response.decision_type);          // "respond" | "propose_action" | ...

The ChatResponse type carries the full response shape including reply, trust_boundary, segments, decision_type, and audit_trail. All fields are typed.

Send to a specific nest

const response = await raptor.chat("proceed", { nestId: "nest-uuid" });

4. Read trust boundaries with TypeScript types

The SDK exports trust-boundary types as first-class citizens:

import type {
  TrustBoundary,
  TrustSegment,
  TrustLevel,
  DecisionSupport,
  ConfidenceLevel,
} from "@raptor/sdk";

Trust levels form a hierarchy: confirmed > executed > retrieved > inferred > unknown. Every response carries a primary trust level plus per-segment boundaries:

function handleResponse(response: ChatResponse) {
  const boundary: TrustBoundary = response.trust_boundary;

// Primary boundary — the overall trust level for the response  if (bou

Policy Authoring

Governance policies define what operations are allowed, with what capabilities and budgets. Policies are default-deny, Ed25519-signed, and immutable once stored. This guide covers the full lifecycle: write, validate, sign, activate, simulate, and rollback.

Policy structure

A policy document is JSON with three required fields:

{
  "version": "1.0",
  "default": "deny",
  "rules": [
    {
      "match": { "operation": "chat_execution" },
      "action": "allow",
      "capabilities": ["chat", "read_context"],
      "budget": {
        "max_duration_ms": 30000,
        "max_steps": 10,
        "max_provider_calls": 5,
        "max_artifacts": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "match": { "operation": "artifact_generate" },
      "action": "allow",
      "capabilities": ["artifact_write"],
      "budget": { "max_artifacts": 10 }
    },
    {
      "match": { "operation": "" },
      "action": "deny"
    }
  ]
}

| Field | Required | Description ||---|---|---|| version | Yes | Policy format version || default | Yes | Must be "deny" — Raptor enforces default-deny || rules | Yes | At least one rule || rules[].match.operation | Yes | Operation type to match, or "" for wildcard || rules[].action | Yes | "allow" or "deny" || rules[].capabilities | No | Capabilities granted when rule matches || rules[].budget | No | Resource limits for matched operations || rules[].required_approvals | No | Number of human approvals before execution |

Rules are evaluated in order. The first match wins. If no rule matches, the default (deny) applies.

1. Validate

Test your policy without storing or signing it:

curl -X POST https://raptor.harpyits.com/api/admin/policies/validate \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer rpt_your_key" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "policy_json": "{\"version\":\"1.0\",\"default\":\"deny\",\"rules\":[{\"match\":{\"operation\":\"chat_execution\"},\"action\":\"allow\"}]}"
  }'

A valid policy returns:

{
  "ok": true,
  "valid": true,

Chat API

The Chat API is the primary interface for governed AI interaction. Every message passes through the decision engine, governance layer, and trust classifier before a response is returned.

POST /api/chat

Send a governed message. The message is classified, routed through governance, and returned with trust-boundary annotations.

Authentication: Required (any mode)

Request Body

| Field | Type | Required | Description ||-------|------|----------|-------------|| message | string | Yes | The user's message || conversation_id | string | No | Nest ID to scope the conversation. Defaults to "default". |

{
  "message": "What was Q2 revenue and how does it compare to forecast?",
  "conversation_id": "nest_f7a2c9e1"
}

Response Body

{
  "ok": true,
  "reply": "Q2 revenue was $4.2M, which is 7% above the $3.9M forecast.",
  "decision_type": "respond",
  "trust_boundary": {
    "primary": "confirmed",
    "sublabel": "2 of 2 segments verified",
    "actionability": {
      "level": "safe",
      "label": "All claims verified against source"
    },
    "provenance": {
      "execution_id": "exec_8b3f1a2e",
      "input_hash": "sha256:a1b2c3d4..."
    }
  },
  "segments": [
    {
      "boundary": "confirmed",
      "text": "Q2 revenue was $4.2M",
      "basis": "database_query"
    },
    {
      "boundary": "confirmed",
      "text": "7% above the $3.9M forecast",
      "basis": "database_query"
    }
  ],
  "decision_support": {
    "confidence": "CLEAR",
    "recommendation": "Revenue figure verified against financial database.",
    "basis": "Direct query to transactions_summary table, fiscal Q2 2026.",
    "risk_if_wrong": "Financial misreporting. Downstream decisions based on incorrect revenue.",
    "alternatives": []
